Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR)
Respiratory System
Morning hoarseness, Frequent throat clearing, Posterior laryngeal erythema

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-027
Reason for Visit: Frightening, abrupt episode of waking up gasping for air and choking, requiring immediate assessment to rule out airway obstruction or sleep apnea.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who awoke from sleep abruptly gasping, choking, and unable to speak clearly, causing severe panic. Urgent assessment was required to rule out acute laryngospasm secondary to aspiration or primary airway pathology before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I woke up choking and couldn't pull any air in for a terrifying 30 seconds; now my throat feels raw and coated in thick slime.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of frequent, habitual clearing of the throat and a sensation of a persistent lump in the throat (globus). They note hoarseness and vocal fatigue that is particularly prominent in the morning. Tonight, they experienced an episode of abrupt laryngospasm upon waking, gasping for breath. The patient reports a chronic cough that is worsened when lying supine. They frequently experience a bitter taste in the mouth and excessive throat mucus, though they explicitly deny any typical heartburn or dyspepsia (silent reflux).
Functional Status: Fully independent; currently highly anxious but breathing normally.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep anxiety following the choking episode.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 breaths/min | SpO2: 99% on room air | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, but frequently clearing their throat and speaking with a slightly hoarse, gravelly voice.
Physical Exam: Respiratory auscultation is completely clear bilaterally. Oropharyngeal examination reveals a mildly erythematous posterior pharynx. The clinical triad of morning hoarseness, frequent throat clearing, and suspected posterior laryngeal erythema is present. No stridor or respiratory distress is noted at rest. Cardiovascular exam is unremarkable.
Environmental Observations: Patient had eaten a large, late dinner with alcohol shortly before bed.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications; occasional antacids taken rarely.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR) - Status: Exacerbated (Laryngospasm)
Differentials:

Obstructive Sleep Apnea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The primary symptom is a choking/spasm sensation causing raw throat and sour taste, rather than simple apneic pauses reported by the partner, though the two conditions can coexist.

Asthma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear chest auscultation, symptoms localized purely to the throat/larynx, and no daytime wheeze.

Post-Nasal Drip (Allergic Rhinitis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a bitter taste, abrupt laryngospasm, and lack of overt nasal congestion or sneezing.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic "silent reflux" (LPR), culminating in a terrifying episode of nocturnal laryngospasm. The acid regurgitated into the larynx during sleep, triggering a violent protective reflex that briefly closed the vocal cords. The absence of typical heartburn is a hallmark of LPR. Leaving the patient unassessed until the next in-hours period would have resulted in severe, unmanaged panic regarding perceived asphyxiation and the likelihood of recurrent episodes upon returning to sleep. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly justified to perform a normal respiratory exam, definitively diagnose the reflex nature of the choking, and implement immediate behavioral and pharmacological reflux controls.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a high-dose Proton Pump Inhibitor (e.g., Pantoprazole 40mg twice daily) to aggressively suppress gastric acid production.
Positive Behavioral Change: Elevate the head of your entire bed frame by placing 6-inch blocks under the top legs; utilizing gravity physically prevents the stomach acid from flowing upward into your throat while you sleep, directly stopping the acid burns that cause the choking spasms.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bed risers or a medical wedge pillow.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks to assess response to the PPI therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop true difficulty swallowing solid food, if you cough up blood, or if a choking episode does not resolve within 60 seconds and causes you to lose consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: LPR requires strict adherence to dietary and lifestyle changes; medication alone is often insufficient for complete symptom resolution.
Clinical Course: The throat clearing and hoarseness will take several weeks to resolve as the delicate laryngeal tissues slowly heal from the acid burns.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return to the emergency room if food feels physically stuck in your chest when swallowing, or if you experience unexplained weight loss.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Stomach acid is silently traveling up your esophagus while you lie flat, burning your vocal cords and causing them to snap shut in a panic reflex; the medication turns off the acid pumps in your stomach to allow the throat to heal.
Actionable Steps: Stop all food and alcohol intake exactly 3 hours before going to bed; going to sleep with an empty stomach ensures there is no material left to reflux upwards, guaranteeing a safe and uninterrupted sleep.

